{
    "map": {
        "boundary.descr": "The polygon feature or features that will define the area to be processed.",
        "boundary.title": "Processing Boundary",
        "description": "Calculate the displacement between two surfaces to determine where one is above, below or the same as the other surface.",
        "extent.descr": "<xdoc><p>The extent of the data that will be evaluated.</p><ul><li>Current Display Extent—The extent will be based on the active map or scene.</li><li>Draw Extent—The extent will be based on a rectangle drawn on the map or scene.</li><li>Extent of a Layer—The extent will be based on an active map layer. Choose an available layer or use the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Extent of data in all layers</span> option. Each map layer has the following options:<p><ul><li>All Features—The extent of all features.</li><li>Selected Features —The extent of the selected features.</li><li>Visible Features—The extent of visible features.</li></ul></p></li><li>Browse—The extent will be based on a dataset.</li><li>Intersection of Inputs—The extent will be the intersecting extent of all inputs.</li><li>Union of Inputs—The extent will be the combined extent of all inputs.</li><li>Clipboard—The extent can be copied to and from the clipboard.<ul><li>Copy Extent—Copies the extent and coordinate system to the clipboard.</li><li>Paste Extent—Pastes the extent and coordinate system from the clipboard. If the clipboard does not include a coordinate system, the extent will use the map’s coordinate system.</li></ul></li><li>Reset Extent—The extent will be reset to the default value.</li></ul><p>When coordinates are manually provided, the coordinates must be numeric values and in the active map&apos;s coordinate system. The map may use different display units than the provided coordinates. Use a negative value sign for south and west coordinates.</p></xdoc>",
        "extent.title": "Processing Extent",
        "in_reference_surface.descr": "The triangulated surface that will be used as the baseline for determining the relative displacement of the input surface.",
        "in_reference_surface.title": "Reference Surface",
        "in_surface.descr": "The triangulated surface whose relative displacement is being evaluated from the reference surface.",
        "in_surface.title": "Input Surface",
        "method.descr": "<xdoc><p>The method used to select a LAS point in each analysis window when applying an analysis resolution to thin the input LAS dataset surface. The resulting points will be used to construct a triangulated surface.</p><ul><li><span title=\"CLOSEST_TO_MEAN\">Closest to mean</span>—The LAS point whose value is closest to the mean of all LAS points in the analysis window will be used. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"MIN\">Minimum</span>—The LAS point with the smallest z-value among all the LAS points in the analysis window.</li><li><span title=\"MAX\">Maximum</span>—The LAS point with the highest z-value among all the LAS points in the analysis window.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "method.domain.CLOSEST_TO_MEAN": "Closest to mean",
        "method.domain.MAX": "Maximum",
        "method.domain.MIN": "Minimum",
        "method.title": "LAS Thinning Method",
        "out_feature_class.descr": "The output feature class containing contiguous triangles and triangle parts that have the same classification grouped into polygons. The volume enclosed by each region of difference is listed in the attribute table.",
        "out_feature_class.title": "Output Feature Class",
        "out_raster.descr": "The output raster surface whose values represent the input surface normalized against the reference surface. Positive values reflect areas where the input surface is above the reference surface, whereas negative values indicate the areas where the input surface is below the reference surface. The raster's values are derived from a TIN using linear interpolation.",
        "out_raster.title": "Output Raster",
        "out_tin_basename.descr": "The base name given to each output TIN surface. If one TIN dataset is not sufficient to represent the data, multiple TINs will be created with the same base name.",
        "out_tin_basename.title": "Output TIN Base Name",
        "out_tin_folder.descr": "The folder location for storing one or more TIN surfaces whose values represent the difference between the input and reference surface.",
        "out_tin_folder.title": "Output TIN Folder",
        "params.category1": "Raster Options",
        "params.category2": "TIN Options",
        "params.category3": "Processing Extent",
        "pyramid_level_resolution.descr": "The resolution that will be used to generate the input surface. For a terrain dataset, this will correspond to its pyramid-level definitions, where the default of 0 represents full resolution. For a LAS dataset, this value represents the length of each side of the square area that will be used to thin the LAS point returns.",
        "pyramid_level_resolution.title": "Analysis Resolution",
        "raster_cell_size.descr": "The cell size of the output raster.",
        "raster_cell_size.title": "Raster Cell Size",
        "reference_method.descr": "<xdoc><p>The method used to select a LAS point in each analysis window when applying an analysis resolution to thin the input LAS dataset surface. The resulting points will be used to construct a triangulated surface.</p><ul><li><span title=\"CLOSEST_TO_MEAN\">Closest to mean</span>—The LAS point whose value is closest to the mean of all LAS points in the analysis window will be used. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"MIN\">Minimum</span>—The LAS point with the smallest z-value among all the LAS points in the analysis window.</li><li><span title=\"MAX\">Maximum</span>—The LAS point with the highest z-value among all the LAS points in the analysis window.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "reference_method.domain.CLOSEST_TO_MEAN": "Closest to mean",
